preferred-citation:
  type: unpublished
  title: "VortFoil: A Vortex-Panel-Informed Neural Operator for Steady Airfoil Flow Prediction"
  notes: "Submitted."
  authors:
    - family-names: Rhie
      given-names: Sang-Hyun
      affiliation: "Kyung Hee University"
    - family-names: Kim
      given-names: Gyuwon
      affiliation: "Kyung Hee University"
    - family-names: Lim
      given-names: Suhyeong
      affiliation: "Kyung Hee University"
    - family-names: Lee
      given-names: Jeongsu
      affiliation: "Kyung Hee University"
      email: leejeongsu@khu.ac.kr
  abstract: >-
    VortFoil links aerodynamic coefficients to predicted states through a fixed-resolution
    neural operator. Network-predicted panelwise rates are projected onto a fixed Fourier
    basis and analytically integrated to form an effective vortex sheet. A projection
    enforces the discrete Kutta condition, and panel readouts provide surface pressure,
    circulation lift, moment, and stagnation location without stagnation-position labels. A
    boundary-layer operator predicts thickness profiles in parallel from the same geometry
    and operating conditions. Drag and lift follow from state-conditioned empirical closures
    fitted by ridge regression and frozen at deployment. Selected readouts condition a
    separately trained Reynolds-averaged Navier-Stokes (RANS) field decoder. On the attached
    XFOIL holdout of 80 unseen airfoils, median pressure-coefficient relative L2 and
    stagnation-location errors are 0.100 and 0.77 panels. Drag R^2 is 0.920 (attached set)
    and 0.934 (separated set); NeuralFoil achieves higher lift, drag, and moment R^2 on both
    sets. Trained on the same AirfRANS cases as an XFOIL-conditioned decoder, the
    operator-conditioned field decoder has paired error-ratio 95% confidence intervals,
    relative to that decoder, below unity on u_x, u_y and p; the nu_t interval includes
    unity. It has lower aggregate errors than a published field-operator baseline on u_x, p
    and nu_t, but not on u_y, and its velocity errors above 8 degrees incidence exceed that
    baseline's published aggregate. Surface lift and drag shape gradients are checked
    against finite differences of the same single-precision forward map.
# [AUTHOR] add the paper's doi to preferred-citation once it is published.
